1. **FS-8A — blocker: parent-directory aliasing remains possible.**  
   File: [replacement items.jsonl](.agent/heldout-check/fs8fix/items.jsonl)  
   Evidence: `"hold/ledger.csv same-file data/ledger.csv"` and both paths report `"regular file, 2 links"`.

   These observations also fit `hold -> data`, with an additional hard link elsewhere supplying the link count. `stat` follows symlinks in parent components. After `sed -i` replaces `data/ledger.csv`, both displayed paths then resolve to the **new** inode. The restore copies a file onto itself; the original bytes are not restored. That permits `lost=["R2"], recoverable=[], permanent_loss=true`, contradicting the candidate answer while preserving every displayed artifact.

   **Minimal fix:** show `ls -ld data hold`, establishing that both parents are ordinary directories.

2. **FS-8A, FS-8B, R-10A, R-10B — blocker: write permissions are unspecified.**  
   Files: [FS-8 replacement](.agent/heldout-check/fs8fix/items.jsonl), [v2 items](.agent/heldout-check/v2/items.jsonl)  
   Evidence: `"The command runs as a non-root user"`; FS-8 prints only `"%n: %F, %h links"`; R-10 uses `": > volumes/pgdata/ledger.seg"` without showing permissions.

   Mode `0444` produces identical displayed evidence:
   - FS-8A: with writable parents, `sed -i` replaces the file and preserves its mode; the subsequent plain `cp` cannot overwrite it. R2 becomes unrecoverable. [GNU sed implementation](https://github.com/mirror/sed/blob/v4.9/sed/execute.c#L603-L637)
   - FS-8B: redirection into the ledger fails, leaving no lost resource.
   - R-10A/B: truncation fails, also leaving no lost resource.

   **Minimal fix:** add a shared environment statement that permissions permit the shown operations unless explicitly displayed otherwise, or supply the relevant ownership and permission evidence.

